import hashlib
import json

def sha256(text: str) -> str:
    return hashlib.sha256(text.encode('utf-8')).hexdigest()

day37 = {'day': 37, 'title': 'Lane Priority Architecture: Bitmask Priority Scheduling (Sync, Continuous, Default, Idle Lanes)', 'desc': "Master React's 31-bit Lane priority model: explore the mathematical transition from numeric priorities to bitwise bitmasks, understand least-significant bit extraction (lanes & -lanes), lane merging, and priority starvation escalation.", 'minutes': 38, 'parts': [{'title': 'From Numeric Priorities to Bitmasks: The Limitations of Integer Priority Levels', 'say': ["In our previous session on Day 36, we explored the mechanics of cooperative time-slicing and how React's scheduler divides work into 5ms macro-task slices using MessageChannel. However, a scheduler that only understands time-slicing cannot answer a fundamental operational question: when multiple competing state updates are queued simultaneously, which update must be processed first, which updates can be bundled together, and which updates can be safely deferred? In early architectures of Concurrent React (specifically during the React 16 and 17 alpha eras), the core team attempted to model update priorities using scalar numeric levels—such as ImmediatePriority (1), UserBlockingPriority (2), NormalPriority (3), LowPriority (4), and IdlePriority (5).", 'While scalar integers appeared intuitive on the surface, they broke down completely under complex real-world application workflows. In high-performance web applications, updates do not arrive in a simple linear priority queue where each task has a single priority value. Often, an ongoing background data fetch (LowPriority) might share state dependencies with an urgent user text input (ImmediatePriority), or multiple independent background transitions might need to be evaluated in parallel without blocking one another. With scalar integers, representing a task that simultaneously belongs to multiple priority categories requires complex set data structures, hash maps, or dynamic array allocations, all of which introduce severe garbage collection churn and algorithmic overhead in the hot render loop.', "Furthermore, scalar priorities made it mathematically impossible to express decoupled 'lanes' of work. If task A had priority 3 and task B had priority 3, an integer-based scheduler could not distinguish between two independent features both running at normal priority versus two updates that were explicitly intended to be batched together into a single atomic commit. If a user initiated a tab switch while a data table was filtering, an integer scheduler would be forced to either merge them blindly into a single gigantic render or cancel the first one entirely, causing wasted compute and visual flashing.", "To overcome these architectural bottlenecks, React 18 introduced a complete redesign of the scheduling priority model: the 31-Bit Bitmask Lane Architecture. Instead of representing priority as a scalar integer from 1 to 5, React models priorities as a set of 31 discrete binary bits within a 32-bit signed integer. Each individual bit represents a distinct 'lane' of execution. Because modern JavaScript engines like Google Chrome's V8 optimize 31-bit integers as Small Integers (SMIs) stored directly on the stack without heap allocation, bitmask operations execute in a single CPU clock cycle, delivering lightning-fast priority calculations with zero memory allocations.", 'In the executable demonstration below, we compare the memory and computational complexity of tracking multi-priority tasks using traditional JavaScript Sets versus 31-bit binary bitmasks. Notice how combining multiple priority lanes with bitwise OR takes a fraction of the memory and executes orders of magnitude faster than allocating dynamic collections. Run the code to observe the foundational power of bitwise priority modeling.', 'To fully understand the shift from scalar numbers to bitmasks, we must examine how JavaScript engines execute priority checks inside the inner loop of the Fiber reconciler. In an enterprise web application with thousands of components, the work loop checks whether the current Fiber node requires rendering millions of times during a single user session. When priorities were represented as numeric ranges or Set objects, every priority check required property lookups, function calls, or iterative loops. In high-frequency rendering scenarios, the CPU spent substantial time running priority arithmetic rather than executing application code.', 'By contrast, bitwise operations on 31-bit integers are native CPU instructions executed directly within CPU registers. When the V8 engine compiles a bitwise expression like `(pendingLanes & renderLanes) !== 0`, it emits a single assembly instruction: a bitwise AND followed by a zero-flag test (e.g. `TEST EAX, EBX` on x86-64 architectures). There is zero memory indirection, zero cache misses, and zero heap allocation. This bare-metal efficiency allows React to evaluate subtree priority bails in less than a nanosecond per Fiber node.'], 'code': "// Comparing Scalar Priority Sets vs 31-Bit Binary Bitmasks\nconst PRIORITY_IMMEDIATE = 1;\nconst PRIORITY_USER_BLOCKING = 2;\nconst PRIORITY_NORMAL = 3;\nconst PRIORITY_IDLE = 5;\n\n// Approach A: Legacy Object / Set based priority aggregation\nconst taskA_priorities = new Set([PRIORITY_USER_BLOCKING, PRIORITY_NORMAL]);\nfunction hasPrioritySet(taskSet, priority) {\n  return taskSet.has(priority);\n}\n\n// Approach B: React 18/19 31-Bit Lane Bitmasks\nconst SyncLane = 0b0001;            // Bit 0: Urgent discrete inputs (clicks, keypresses)\nconst InputContinuousLane = 0b0010; // Bit 1: Continuous inputs (scroll, drag, mousemove)\nconst DefaultLane = 0b0100;         // Bit 2: Normal state updates (fetch resolution)\nconst IdleLane = 0b1000;            // Bit 3: Offscreen / background idle computation\n\n// Merge lanes using bitwise OR (|)\nconst taskB_lanes = InputContinuousLane | DefaultLane; // 0b0110\n\n// Test lane membership using bitwise AND (&)\nfunction hasLaneBitmask(taskLanes, targetLane) {\n  return (taskLanes & targetLane) !== 0;\n}\n\nconsole.log('--- Set vs Bitmask Evaluation ---');\nconsole.log('Set Contains Normal Priority: ' + hasPrioritySet(taskA_priorities, PRIORITY_NORMAL));\nconsole.log('Bitmask Contains DefaultLane: ' + hasLaneBitmask(taskB_lanes, DefaultLane));\nconsole.log('Bitmask Binary Representation: 0b' + taskB_lanes.toString(2).padStart(4, '0'));\nconsole.log('Bitmask Contains SyncLane: ' + hasLaneBitmask(taskB_lanes, SyncLane));\n", 'output': '--- Set vs Bitmask Evaluation ---\nSet Contains Normal Priority: true\nBitmask Contains DefaultLane: true\nBitmask Binary Representation: 0b0110\nBitmask Contains SyncLane: false', 'codeNotes': [{'line': 2, 'note': 'Legacy systems used numeric scalars or Set collections, allocating heap objects.'}, {'line': 12, 'note': 'React Lanes use single bits: SyncLane (0b0001), InputContinuous (0b0010), etc.'}, {'line': 18, 'note': 'Bitwise OR (|) merges multiple lanes into a single Small Integer (SMI) in 1 CPU cycle.'}, {'line': 21, 'note': 'Bitwise AND (&) tests lane membership instantly with zero memory allocation.'}], 'tryIt': 'Add an extra lane (OffscreenLane = 0b00010000) and merge it with taskB_lanes using bitwise OR.', 'check': {'question': 'Why did React replace scalar integer priority levels (1-5) with 31-bit bitmask lanes?', 'options': ['Because bitmasks allow representing, merging, and filtering multiple concurrent priority categories in a single CPU cycle using Small Integers without heap allocations', 'Because JavaScript deprecated numeric addition in ECMAScript 2020', 'Because integer priorities prevented React from compiling to WebAssembly', 'Because monitors require binary numbers to display colors'], 'answer': 0, 'why': 'Bitmasks enable representing sets of priority categories as 31-bit integers on the stack, allowing instant bitwise union, intersection, and subtraction without garbage collection overhead.'}}, {'title': 'The 31 Lanes Taxonomy: Discrete, Continuous, Default, Transition & Idle Lanes', 'say': ['Now that we understand why bitmasks are mathematically superior to scalar integers, let us explore the complete taxonomy of lanes utilized by the React reconciler. In the React source code (packages/react-reconciler/src/ReactFiberLane.js), 31 distinct bits are defined within a signed 32-bit integer. The 32nd bit is reserved for the sign bit in standard ECMAScript integers, so React uses exactly 31 usable bits. These 31 bits are systematically grouped into semantic categories representing different degrees of user urgency and computational intent.', 'At the very top of the hierarchy sits SyncLane (bit 0: 0b0000000000000000000000000000001). SyncLane represents discrete user interactions that demand immediate, synchronous execution before the browser can paint the very next frame. Examples include typing characters into a controlled text input, clicking a toggle button, or submitting a form. When an update is scheduled in SyncLane, React bypasses cooperative time-slicing and processes the Fiber tree synchronously to prevent any perceptible input latency or typing stutter.', 'Immediately following SyncLane are the InputContinuousLanes (such as bit 1: 0b0010). These lanes represent high-frequency user interactions that continuously emit events, such as scrolling a container, dragging a slider, or tracking mouse movements across a canvas. These updates are urgent, but unlike discrete clicks, individual intermediate frames during a drag or scroll can be coalesced if the device GPU is under heavy load, ensuring smooth motion without choking the event pipeline.', 'Next in the hierarchy is DefaultLane (bit 2: 0b0100). DefaultLane is where standard state updates originate—such as resolving a fetch() promise, processing a WebSocket event, or dispatching an unadorned setState inside a custom hook. Following DefaultLane are the TransitionLanes (bits 6 through 21). There are 16 dedicated TransitionLanes in React. These lanes represent non-urgent background rendering scheduled via startTransition or useTransition. Because there are 16 separate transition lanes, React can manage up to 16 concurrent transitions independently without having them interfere with one another.', 'At the lowest end of the priority spectrum sit RetryLanes (used for suspended asynchronous boundaries) and IdleLane (bit 29: 0b0010000000000000000000000000000). IdleLane updates only execute when the user is completely inactive, the browser has zero pending events, and there is surplus frame headroom. For instance, pre-rendering offscreen tabs or warming an analytics cache is scheduled into IdleLane. By segregating updates into this granular 31-bit taxonomy, React can juggle dozens of simultaneous operations while keeping the main UI thread silky smooth.', 'Study the taxonomy classifier script below. We construct an enterprise LaneClassifier class that maps arbitrary lane bitmasks to their semantic category, priority weight, and human-readable description. Notice how bitwise operations make classification instant and unambiguous. Run the code to trace how React categorizes different application events.', 'A deeper inspection of the 31-bit lane taxonomy reveals how React differentiates between discrete and continuous user interactions. Discrete interactions—such as a user pressing a keyboard key or clicking a navigation link—represent intentional, state-altering commitments. The user expects the typed letter or button state to update immediately on screen. Dropping an intermediate character or delaying a button click feels broken and laggy. Therefore, discrete events are mapped to SyncLane, guaranteeing immediate, un-interruptible processing.', "Conversely, continuous interactions—such as dragging a modal slider, pinching to zoom, or scrolling a massive feed—emit dozens or hundreds of high-frequency events per second. In a continuous stream, the user's eye cannot perceive every individual mouse position; rather, the user cares about smooth visual continuity. If the device GPU or CPU falls behind, the reconciler can safely coalesce or drop intermediate frames in InputContinuousLane without violating user expectations. By decoupling discrete SyncLane from InputContinuousLane, React ensures that a fast-typing user never suffers latency even while heavy background animations or smooth scrolling are active."], 'code': "// React 31-Bit Lane Taxonomy & Priority Classification\nconst TotalLanes = 31;\n\n// Core Lane Definitions (represented as binary bitmasks)\nconst SyncLane               = 0b00000001; // Bit 0: Urgent discrete inputs (clicks, typing)\nconst InputContinuousLane    = 0b00000010; // Bit 1: Continuous inputs (scroll, drag)\nconst DefaultLane            = 0b00000100; // Bit 2: Standard async state updates\nconst TransitionLane1        = 0b00001000; // Bit 3: Concurrent Transition #1\nconst TransitionLane2        = 0b00010000; // Bit 4: Concurrent Transition #2\nconst RetryLane              = 0b00100000; // Bit 5: Suspended component retries\nconst IdleLane               = 0b01000000; // Bit 6: Background idle work\n\nfunction classifyLane(lane) {\n  if (lane & SyncLane) {\n    return { name: 'SyncLane', priority: 'Urgent/Synchronous', maxBudgetMs: 0 };\n  }\n  if (lane & InputContinuousLane) {\n    return { name: 'InputContinuousLane', priority: 'High Continuous', maxBudgetMs: 5 };\n  }\n  if (lane & DefaultLane) {\n    return { name: 'DefaultLane', priority: 'Normal', maxBudgetMs: 50 };\n  }\n  if (lane & (TransitionLane1 | TransitionLane2)) {\n    return { name: 'TransitionLane', priority: 'Low/Interruptible', maxBudgetMs: 5000 };\n  }\n  if (lane & IdleLane) {\n    return { name: 'IdleLane', priority: 'Idle/Offscreen', maxBudgetMs: 10000 };\n  }\n  return { name: 'UnknownLane', priority: 'None', maxBudgetMs: Infinity };\n}\n\n// Inspect 4 real-world UI actions\nconst action1 = SyncLane;                       // User presses keyboard key\nconst action2 = InputContinuousLane;            // User drags map view\nconst action3 = DefaultLane;                    // Server responds with JSON\nconst action4 = TransitionLane1 | TransitionLane2; // User switches tabs\n\nconsole.log('Action 1 (Typing): ' + classifyLane(action1).name + ' [' + classifyLane(action1).priority + ']');\nconsole.log('Action 2 (Scroll): ' + classifyLane(action2).name + ' [' + classifyLane(action2).priority + ']');\nconsole.log('Action 3 (API Res): ' + classifyLane(action3).name + ' [' + classifyLane(action3).priority + ']');\nconsole.log('Action 4 (Tab Sw): ' + classifyLane(action4).name + ' [' + classifyLane(action4).priority + ']');\n", 'output': 'Action 1 (Typing): SyncLane [Urgent/Synchronous]\nAction 2 (Scroll): InputContinuousLane [High Continuous]\nAction 3 (API Res): DefaultLane [Normal]\nAction 4 (Tab Sw): TransitionLane [Low/Interruptible]', 'codeNotes': [{'line': 5, 'note': 'SyncLane (bit 0) has 0ms budget; it executes immediately without yielding.'}, {'line': 6, 'note': 'InputContinuousLane (bit 1) handles scrolls and drags without blocking the UI.'}, {'line': 8, 'note': 'TransitionLanes (bits 3-4) have generous budgets and are fully interruptible.'}, {'line': 15, 'note': 'classifyLane inspects bits using bitwise AND to return priority metadata.'}], 'tryIt': 'Add a check for IdleLane in action5 and verify that it classifies with Idle/Offscreen priority.', 'check': {'question': "Which lane in React's 31-bit taxonomy is reserved for urgent discrete user interactions like keyboard typing?", 'options': ['SyncLane (Bit 0)', 'IdleLane (Bit 29)', 'TransitionLane (Bit 6)', 'RetryLane (Bit 5)'], 'answer': 0, 'why': 'SyncLane (Bit 0) represents the highest priority in React and is executed synchronously to guarantee zero typing latency.'}}, {'title': 'Bitwise Math in the Reconciler: Least-Significant Bit Extraction (lanes & -lanes)', 'say': ['Now we arrive at one of the most intellectually elegant algorithmic techniques in the entire React codebase: how does the Fiber reconciler pick the single highest-priority lane from a bitmask containing multiple queued lanes? Suppose a component has accumulated pending work from SyncLane (0b0001), DefaultLane (0b0100), and IdleLane (0b1000). The merged bitmask stored on the Fiber root is 0b1101. To begin rendering, the reconciler must isolate the single highest-priority bit (which corresponds to the lowest numeric bit position, bit 0). How can this be accomplished without running a slow for-loop or inspecting bits individually?', "The answer lies in two's complement binary arithmetic and the famous bitwise identity: lanes & -lanes. In modern computing systems, negative numbers are represented using two's complement. To negate a number in two's complement, you invert all bits (bitwise NOT) and add 1 (-x = ~x + 1). When you invert a binary number and add 1, all bits higher than the lowest set bit are inverted, but the lowest set bit and all trailing zeros are preserved. Therefore, performing a bitwise AND between a number and its negation (lanes & -lanes) isolates the single least-significant set bit in exactly one CPU instruction!", "Let us trace this with concrete binary numbers. Consider lanes = 12, which in binary is 0b1100 (bits 2 and 3 are set). Its bitwise NOT is ~12, where 0 becomes 1 and 1 becomes 0. Adding 1 gives -12. When we perform 12 & -12, the result is 0b0100 (which is 4, bit 2). The reconciler instantly isolated bit 2—the highest-priority pending lane—in a single clock cycle without any loops, arrays, or conditional branches. In React's source code, this operation is encapsulated in the getHighestPriorityLane(lanes) function.", 'Beyond isolating the highest priority lane, the reconciler relies on three other standard bitwise operations throughout the work loop. To add a newly scheduled update to the pending lanes, it performs a bitwise union: pendingLanes |= newLane. To test whether a specific Fiber subtree contains pending work in the currently active lane, it performs a bitwise intersection: (subtreeLanes & renderLane) !== 0. Finally, once a lane has been successfully committed to the DOM, the reconciler clears that lane using bitwise subtraction: pendingLanes &= ~renderLane.', "Review the interactive bitwise arithmetic simulation below. We demonstrate two's complement negation, least-significant bit extraction using lanes & -lanes, lane addition, and lane removal. Run the script to verify how low-level bitwise operations power the concurrent reconciler.", "The mathematical elegance of two's complement least-significant bit extraction (`lanes & -lanes`) is rooted in digital logic design. In computer systems, two's complement arithmetic allows addition and subtraction to use identical hardware circuitry. When a positive binary integer `x` is negated to `-x`, the bitwise inversion (`~x`) flips all bits, changing the trailing zeros to ones and the lowest set bit to zero. Adding 1 triggers a carry ripple that propagates across the inverted trailing ones until it reaches the inverted lowest bit, flipping it back to one and resetting all trailing bits to zero. All higher bits remain the inverse of `x`.", 'When the bitwise AND (`x & -x`) is evaluated, every bit above the lowest set bit is ANDed with its inverse, resulting in zero. The bits below the lowest set bit were zeros initially, so they remain zero. Only the lowest set bit itself—which is 1 in both `x` and `-x`—evaluates to 1. Consequently, `lanes & -lanes` isolates the lowest set bit in exactly one machine cycle. Because React assigns its highest priorities to the lowest bit indices (bit 0 = SyncLane, bit 1 = ContinuousLane, bit 2 = DefaultLane), extracting the least significant bit automatically yields the highest priority task awaiting execution.'], 'code': "// The Magic of Bitwise Math in React: Least-Significant Bit Extraction\nfunction getHighestPriorityLane(lanes) {\n  // Two's complement identity: isolates the lowest set bit in 1 instruction\n  return lanes & -lanes;\n}\n\n// Define test lanes\nconst LaneA = 0b00000001; // Bit 0 (SyncLane - Highest Priority)\nconst LaneB = 0b00000100; // Bit 2 (DefaultLane - Normal Priority)\nconst LaneC = 0b00010000; // Bit 4 (TransitionLane - Low Priority)\n\n// Scenario 1: Multiple lanes are pending simultaneously\nlet pendingLanes = LaneB | LaneC; // 0b00010100 (Default + Transition)\nconsole.log('Initial Pending Lanes: 0b' + pendingLanes.toString(2).padStart(8, '0'));\n\n// Reconciler selects highest priority pending lane\nlet activeRenderLane = getHighestPriorityLane(pendingLanes);\nconsole.log('Selected Highest Priority Lane: 0b' + activeRenderLane.toString(2).padStart(8, '0') + ' (Bit 2: DefaultLane)');\n\n// Scenario 2: Urgent Sync update arrives while rendering!\nconsole.log('--- Urgent SyncLane Update Arrives ---');\npendingLanes |= LaneA; // Add LaneA via bitwise OR\nconsole.log('Updated Pending Lanes: 0b' + pendingLanes.toString(2).padStart(8, '0'));\n\n// Reconciler re-evaluates: SyncLane instantly preempts DefaultLane!\nactiveRenderLane = getHighestPriorityLane(pendingLanes);\nconsole.log('Preempted! New Active Lane: 0b' + activeRenderLane.toString(2).padStart(8, '0') + ' (Bit 0: SyncLane)');\n\n// Scenario 3: Commit SyncLane and clear it from pending lanes\nconsole.log('--- Committing SyncLane ---');\npendingLanes &= ~activeRenderLane; // Clear bit via bitwise AND with NOT\nconsole.log('Remaining Pending Lanes: 0b' + pendingLanes.toString(2).padStart(8, '0'));\n", 'output': 'Initial Pending Lanes: 0b00010100\nSelected Highest Priority Lane: 0b00000100 (Bit 2: DefaultLane)\n--- Urgent SyncLane Update Arrives ---\nUpdated Pending Lanes: 0b00010101\nPreempted! New Active Lane: 0b00000001 (Bit 0: SyncLane)\n--- Committing SyncLane ---\nRemaining Pending Lanes: 0b00010100', 'codeNotes': [{'line': 2, 'note': "lanes & -lanes leverages two's complement arithmetic to isolate the lowest set bit."}, {'line': 12, 'note': 'Initial pending lanes merge DefaultLane and TransitionLane (0b00010100).'}, {'line': 20, 'note': 'When LaneA (Sync) arrives, bitwise OR merges it instantly into pendingLanes.'}, {'line': 28, 'note': 'pendingLanes &= ~activeLane strips the completed lane, leaving the remaining lanes.'}], 'tryIt': 'Add LaneD = 0b00000010 (InputContinuousLane) and verify that it preempts DefaultLane but yields to SyncLane.', 'check': {'question': "What mathematical expression does React's reconciler use to isolate the single highest-priority lane from a bitmask in one CPU operation?", 'options': ['lanes & -lanes', 'lanes | ~lanes', 'Math.max(...lanes)', "lanes.split('').reverse()[0]"], 'answer': 0, 'why': "Using two's complement binary representation, lanes & -lanes isolates the lowest set bit (least-significant bit), which represents the highest priority lane."}}, {'title': 'Lane Starvation and Priority Escalation: Expiration Timestamps and Sync Promotion', 'say': ['While the bitmask lane architecture provides exquisite control over update preemption, it introduces a classic concurrent systems challenge: priority starvation. Imagine a scenario where a user is continuously typing rapidly into an input field or scrolling vigorously down a page. Each keystroke dispatches a SyncLane or InputContinuousLane update. If the scheduler strictly prioritizes the highest-priority lane on every cycle, lower-priority updates—such as a background data fetch in DefaultLane or a complex visual transition in TransitionLane—might be starved indefinitely, never receiving CPU execution time.', 'To prevent low-priority updates from starving forever, React implements a priority escalation mechanism driven by expiration timestamps. When any update is scheduled into a lane, React does not merely set a bit in a bitmask. It also calculates a deadline timestamp: expirationTime = currentTime + laneTimeoutMs. For example, a DefaultLane update might be assigned an expiration window of 500 milliseconds, while a TransitionLane update is assigned an expiration window of 5,000 milliseconds (5 seconds).', "During each pass through the work loop, before picking the highest-priority lane via lanes & -lanes, the scheduler audits all pending lanes against the current clock: markStarvedLanesAsExpired(). If the current timestamp has surpassed the expiration timestamp of any pending lane, that lane is deemed 'expired'. The scheduler immediately promotes that starved lane to the expiredLanes bitmask. In React, expired lanes are treated with the exact same urgency as SyncLane: they can no longer be interrupted, and they execute synchronously to completion without time-slicing.", 'This expiration algorithm ensures that React achieves the best of both worlds. Under normal conditions when the user interface is calm, background transitions yield cooperatively and execute in the background with zero impact on frame rates. But if the main thread experiences heavy input contention, background tasks will never freeze or hang permanently; the moment their deadline expires, they escalate to synchronous priority, commit their changes, and clear their lane. This graceful degradation guarantees predictable system liveness across any device workload.', 'Study the starvation escalation engine below. We define a scheduler that registers two updates: an urgent continuous scroll and a background transition. We simulate the passage of virtual time and observe how the transition update—initially deferred—reaches its expiration deadline, escalates to expired status, and preempts all other work. Run the code to trace priority escalation in action.', 'The priority escalation algorithm addresses the classical problem of starvation in priority-based preemptive schedulers. In operating system theory, if a high-priority process continuously consumes CPU cycles, lower-priority background processes may never run. In a frontend UI, this could manifest as an analytical beacon never firing, a background data prefetch never resolving, or a non-urgent visual transition remaining in an eternal pending state while the user interacts with the page.', 'React solves this by binding every lane to an expiration clock. When an update is scheduled, its deadline is calculated using `performance.now() + timeout`. For normal default updates, this deadline is typically 500ms; for transition updates, it is 5,000ms. On every tick of the scheduler work loop, `markStarvedLanesAsExpired()` compares the current time against all pending lane deadlines. Any lane whose deadline has passed is ORed into `root.expiredLanes`. The work loop always checks `expiredLanes` before `pendingLanes`. When an expired lane is detected, React switches from interruptible concurrent rendering to synchronous execution, forcing the starved update to finish immediately. This ensures that while React favors user responsiveness, it never sacrifices eventual consistency.', 'In addition to protecting against continuous user interactions, expiration timestamps serve as a fail-safe mechanism against pathological rendering edge cases. In highly dynamic applications with streaming real-time subscriptions, WebSocket pushes, and recurring timers, background transitions could theoretically be interrupted indefinitely if new higher-priority work is continuously queued. By converting starved tasks into synchronous executions at the deadline, React ensures that user interface state converges toward consistency within a bounded, predictable timeframe under all conditions.'], 'code': "// Priority Starvation and Expiration Escalation Engine\nclass LaneStarvationScheduler {\n  constructor() {\n    this.pendingLanes = 0;\n    this.expiredLanes = 0;\n    this.expirationTimes = {};\n    this.currentTime = 1000; // Simulated virtual start clock\n  }\n\n  // Schedule an update in a specific lane with a timeout\n  scheduleLane(lane, timeoutMs) {\n    this.pendingLanes |= lane;\n    this.expirationTimes[lane] = this.currentTime + timeoutMs;\n    console.log('[Schedule] Lane 0b' + lane.toString(2) + ' scheduled. Expires at: ' + this.expirationTimes[lane] + 'ms');\n  }\n\n  // Advance virtual time and audit for starved lanes\n  advanceTimeAndCheck(elapsedMs) {\n    this.currentTime += elapsedMs;\n    console.log('\\n--- Virtual Clock Advanced to ' + this.currentTime + 'ms ---');\n\n    // Check each pending lane for expiration\n    for (const laneStr in this.expirationTimes) {\n      const lane = Number(laneStr);\n      if ((this.pendingLanes & lane) !== 0) {\n        if (this.currentTime >= this.expirationTimes[lane]) {\n          // Promote starved lane to expired (Sync) status!\n          this.expiredLanes |= lane;\n          console.log('[ESCALATION] Lane 0b' + lane.toString(2) + ' has EXPIRED! Promoted to synchronous urgency!');\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  getNextWorkLane() {\n    // If any lanes have expired, execute them immediately\n    if (this.expiredLanes !== 0) {\n      const urgentLane = this.expiredLanes & -this.expiredLanes;\n      return { lane: urgentLane, isExpired: true };\n    }\n    // Otherwise execute normal highest-priority lane\n    const normalLane = this.pendingLanes & -this.pendingLanes;\n    return { lane: normalLane, isExpired: false };\n  }\n}\n\nconst scheduler = new LaneStarvationScheduler();\nconst ContinuousLane = 0b0010; // Scroll input (timeout: 100ms)\nconst TransitionLane = 0b1000; // Background chart render (timeout: 500ms)\n\nscheduler.scheduleLane(ContinuousLane, 100);\nscheduler.scheduleLane(TransitionLane, 500);\n\n// Clock advances 200ms: ContinuousLane expires and runs\nscheduler.advanceTimeAndCheck(200);\nlet task = scheduler.getNextWorkLane();\nconsole.log('Next Task to Run: Lane 0b' + task.lane.toString(2) + ' (Expired: ' + task.isExpired + ')');\n\n// Clock advances another 400ms (total 1600ms): TransitionLane expires!\nscheduler.advanceTimeAndCheck(400);\ntask = scheduler.getNextWorkLane();\nconsole.log('Next Task to Run: Lane 0b' + task.lane.toString(2) + ' (Expired: ' + task.isExpired + ')');\n", 'output': '[Schedule] Lane 0b10 scheduled. Expires at: 1100ms\n[Schedule] Lane 0b1000 scheduled. Expires at: 1500ms\n\n--- Virtual Clock Advanced to 1200ms ---\n[ESCALATION] Lane 0b10 has EXPIRED! Promoted to synchronous urgency!\nNext Task to Run: Lane 0b10 (Expired: true)\n\n--- Virtual Clock Advanced to 1600ms ---\n[ESCALATION] Lane 0b10 has EXPIRED! Promoted to synchronous urgency!\n[ESCALATION] Lane 0b1000 has EXPIRED! Promoted to synchronous urgency!\nNext Task to Run: Lane 0b10 (Expired: true)', 'codeNotes': [{'line': 9, 'note': 'scheduleLane computes an absolute deadline timestamp for each scheduled lane.'}, {'line': 23, 'note': 'When currentTime >= expirationTime, the starved lane is marked in expiredLanes.'}, {'line': 34, 'note': 'getNextWorkLane checks expiredLanes first, guaranteeing starved tasks run synchronously.'}, {'line': 52, 'note': 'At 1600ms, the background transition lane escalates to prevent indefinite starvation.'}], 'tryIt': 'Adjust the TransitionLane timeout to 1000ms and observe how it avoids escalation at 1600ms.', 'check': {'question': 'How does React prevent low-priority background transition lanes from being starved indefinitely by continuous user inputs?', 'options': ['By assigning expiration timestamps to lanes and promoting starved lanes to synchronous urgency once their deadline expires', 'By crashing the application if an update takes longer than 1 second', 'By deleting background components that take too long to render', 'By ignoring user keyboard and mouse inputs after 100ms'], 'answer': 0, 'why': "React assigns expiration timestamps to each lane. When an update's deadline passes without execution, it is promoted to expired status and executed synchronously."}}, {'title': 'Building a Production 31-Bit Bitmask Lane Scheduler in Pure JavaScript', 'say': ["In this final capstone part of Day 37, we synthesize all concepts learned today into an enterprise-grade 31-Bit Bitmask Lane Scheduler implemented in pure JavaScript. We will incorporate bitwise lane definitions (Sync, Continuous, Default, Transition, Idle), two's complement least-significant bit extraction (lanes & -lanes), lane merging (|), lane subtraction (& ~), expiration timestamp tracking, and priority escalation into a cohesive, deterministic execution engine.", 'Engineering this architecture requires modeling the state transitions of the React Fiber root. The root maintains two primary bitmask registers: pendingLanes (all updates that have been requested but not yet committed) and expiredLanes (updates that exceeded their starvation timeout). When the application calls dispatchAction(lane), the scheduler merges the new lane into pendingLanes, calculates its expiration deadline, and triggers the work loop.', 'During each iteration of the work loop, the scheduler determines the active work lane. If expiredLanes has bits set, it picks the highest expired bit. Otherwise, it picks the highest pending bit via pendingLanes & -pendingLanes. It then processes the corresponding Fiber tree update, logs the execution telemetry, clears the completed lane from pendingLanes and expiredLanes using bitwise negation, and checks whether further work remains.', "Notice the profound architectural advantage of this design: throughout the entire scheduling lifecycle, not a single dynamic array, object allocation, or garbage-collected closure is created inside the critical path. The entire priority state of the multi-tasking application is represented in two 31-bit integer registers operating at the bare-metal speed of CPU registers. This is the secret behind React 18/19's ability to maintain silky smooth 60 and 120 FPS frame rates even during massive synthetic stress tests.", 'Review the complete, fully functioning 31-Bit Bitmask Lane Scheduler below. We configure 4 tasks across diverse priority lanes, execute the scheduler, simulate a preemption event and an expiration escalation, and verify that all lanes resolve in exact priority order. Run the code to verify that our lane scheduler operates with 100% mathematical precision.', 'When architecting production React applications or building custom state management libraries, understanding the 31-bit lane architecture empowers developers to debug complex performance issues with surgical precision. For example, when profiling an application in React DevTools, transitions that appear to take unusually long or freeze the UI can often be traced to lane starvation: a low-priority transition was repeatedly interrupted by discrete sync updates until its 5,000ms expiration deadline was reached, forcing an abrupt synchronous render that caused a visible frame drop.', "Furthermore, modern enterprise state libraries (such as Zustand, Redux Toolkit, and TanStack Query) are engineered with React's lane priority model in mind. They leverage React 18's `useSyncExternalStore` and `startTransition` primitives to ensure their state updates are dispatched into the correct lanes. By aligning your application's architecture with the 31-bit lane model, you ensure that urgent keystrokes remain instantaneous while background computations flow smoothly across available frame headroom without thread contention.", 'To conclude our architecture of the 31-bit bitmask scheduler, consider how this mathematical foundation interfaces with the upcoming concepts of useTransition and useDeferredValue. When a component calls `startTransition(() => setState(...))`, React marks the resulting Fiber update with one of the 16 TransitionLanes. When the work loop encounters this update, it evaluates `lanes & -lanes` and begins rendering. If an urgent discrete event (such as a keypress in SyncLane) arrives before the transition completes, React immediately clears its in-progress transition work, yields to the browser, processes the SyncLane update, and only then resumes the TransitionLane.', 'Mastering this priority model unlocks a profound shift in how frontend engineers reason about user experience. Instead of artificially throttling user input with debounce timers or introducing jarring loading spinners for every operation, developers can treat the UI as a concurrent, multi-lane execution canvas. Fast interactions stay fast, heavy computations yield gracefully, and the entire application behaves with the fluidity and responsiveness of a native operating system desktop application.'], 'code': '// Production 31-Bit Bitmask Lane Scheduler\nclass BitmaskLaneScheduler {\n  constructor() {\n    this.pendingLanes = 0;\n    this.expiredLanes = 0;\n    this.expirationTimes = {};\n    this.virtualClock = 0;\n    this.executionLog = [];\n  }\n\n  // Bitmask definitions\n  static SyncLane            = 0b00000001; // Bit 0\n  static InputContinuousLane = 0b00000010; // Bit 1\n  static DefaultLane         = 0b00000100; // Bit 2\n  static TransitionLane      = 0b00001000; // Bit 3\n  static IdleLane            = 0b00010000; // Bit 4\n\n  schedule(taskName, lane, timeoutMs) {\n    this.pendingLanes |= lane;\n    this.expirationTimes[lane] = this.virtualClock + timeoutMs;\n    console.log(\'[Schedule] "\' + taskName + \'" queued into Lane 0b\' + lane.toString(2));\n  }\n\n  getHighestPriorityLane() {\n    // Expired lanes have absolute priority\n    if (this.expiredLanes !== 0) {\n      return this.expiredLanes & -this.expiredLanes;\n    }\n    // Otherwise pick highest priority pending lane\n    return this.pendingLanes & -this.pendingLanes;\n  }\n\n  auditStarvation(currentClock) {\n    this.virtualClock = currentClock;\n    for (const laneStr in this.expirationTimes) {\n      const lane = Number(laneStr);\n      if ((this.pendingLanes & lane) !== 0 && this.virtualClock >= this.expirationTimes[lane]) {\n        this.expiredLanes |= lane;\n        console.log(\'[Audit] Starvation detected! Lane 0b\' + lane.toString(2) + \' escalated to Expired\');\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  flushNextLane() {\n    const lane = this.getHighestPriorityLane();\n    if (lane === 0) {\n      console.log(\'[WorkLoop] All lanes committed!\');\n      return false;\n    }\n\n    const isExpired = (this.expiredLanes & lane) !== 0;\n    console.log(\'[Execute] Reconciling Lane 0b\' + lane.toString(2) + \' (Urgent/Expired: \' + isExpired + \')\');\n\n    // Clear lane from pending and expired bitmasks\n    this.pendingLanes &= ~lane;\n    this.expiredLanes &= ~lane;\n    return true;\n  }\n}\n\nconst engine = new BitmaskLaneScheduler();\n\n// Queue Default and Transition tasks\nengine.schedule(\'FetchProductCatalog\', BitmaskLaneScheduler.DefaultLane, 500);\nengine.schedule(\'RenderBackgroundChart\', BitmaskLaneScheduler.TransitionLane, 200);\n\n// Urgent user click arrives in SyncLane!\nengine.schedule(\'UserCheckoutClick\', BitmaskLaneScheduler.SyncLane, 0);\n\nconsole.log(\'\\n--- Step 1: Execute Highest Priority Lane ---\');\nengine.flushNextLane(); // Runs SyncLane (Bit 0)\n\nconsole.log(\'\\n--- Step 2: Simulate Clock Advance past Transition timeout ---\');\nengine.auditStarvation(300); // TransitionLane (timeout 200ms) expires!\n\nconsole.log(\'\\n--- Step 3: Flush Remaining Lanes ---\');\nwhile (engine.flushNextLane()) {\n  // Flushes expired TransitionLane, then DefaultLane\n}\n', 'output': '[Schedule] "FetchProductCatalog" queued into Lane 0b100\n[Schedule] "RenderBackgroundChart" queued into Lane 0b1000\n[Schedule] "UserCheckoutClick" queued into Lane 0b1\n\n--- Step 1: Execute Highest Priority Lane ---\n[Execute] Reconciling Lane 0b1 (Urgent/Expired: false)\n\n--- Step 2: Simulate Clock Advance past Transition timeout ---\n[Audit] Starvation detected! Lane 0b1000 escalated to Expired\n\n--- Step 3: Flush Remaining Lanes ---\n[Execute] Reconciling Lane 0b1000 (Urgent/Expired: true)\n[Execute] Reconciling Lane 0b100 (Urgent/Expired: false)\n[WorkLoop] All lanes committed!', 'codeNotes': [{'line': 10, 'note': 'BitmaskLaneScheduler declares bit constants for Sync, Continuous, Default, Transition, Idle.'}, {'line': 21, 'note': 'getHighestPriorityLane checks expiredLanes first, then uses lanes & -lanes.'}, {'line': 39, 'note': 'flushNextLane isolates the highest lane and clears it via bitwise AND with NOT (~lane).'}, {'line': 64, 'note': 'Notice how the expired TransitionLane runs BEFORE DefaultLane due to starvation escalation.'}], 'tryIt': 'Add an InputContinuousLane task and verify how it orders relative to DefaultLane and TransitionLane.', 'check': {'question': 'In a 31-bit bitmask scheduler, why does an expired TransitionLane execute before a non-expired DefaultLane, even though DefaultLane normally has higher priority?', 'options': ['Because expired lanes are escalated to top-priority urgency to prevent indefinite starvation of background tasks', 'Because DefaultLane is automatically deleted if any other lane exists', 'Because TransitionLanes cannot be paused once started', 'Because the JavaScript runtime executes functions in alphabetical order'], 'answer': 0, 'why': 'When a background lane exceeds its starvation timeout, it is promoted into expiredLanes, giving it immediate precedence over non-expired normal lanes.'}}], 'summary': ['React 18/19 replaces scalar integer priorities with a 31-bit bitmask lane architecture, representing priority states as 31-bit Small Integers (SMIs) on the stack.', 'The 31-lane taxonomy groups work into SyncLane (discrete inputs), InputContinuousLane (scroll/drag), DefaultLane (async fetch), TransitionLanes (background renders), and IdleLane (offscreen).', "The reconciler extracts the single highest-priority lane in one CPU instruction using the two's complement identity: lanes & -lanes.", 'Lane merging uses bitwise OR (pending |= lane), testing uses bitwise AND (pending & lane), and clearance uses bitwise NOT (pending &= ~lane).', 'To prevent indefinite priority starvation, React tracks expiration deadlines for each lane, promoting starved background lanes to synchronous urgency once expired.'], 'projectStep': {'title': 'Bitmask Lane Priority Engine', 'steps': ['Define binary bit constants for SyncLane (0b0001), InputContinuousLane (0b0010), DefaultLane (0b0100), and IdleLane (0b1000).', "Implement getHighestPriorityLane(lanes) using the two's complement least-significant bit identity (lanes & -lanes).", 'Maintain pendingLanes and expiredLanes bitmasks on the scheduler root object.', 'Calculate expiration deadlines for each scheduled lane to detect priority starvation.', 'Escalate expired lanes to synchronous execution before processing normal pending lanes.']}}
